finding: The extra computes authority correctly in the tested cases but does not replicate it. The cheap agreement test supplies its own onSend encoder and decoder; the rooms example supplies a different implementation. Removing leadership delivery from the extra cannot fail this test because the extra does not deliver it in the first place. The unit promises replication at the extra while holding the wire fixed; disclosure argues for game opt-in, not for every game to implement its own delivery. Also, private code reservation does not bind the creator to first admission: room-host reserve stores only code/expiry and selectRoom lets any first code-bearing join claim it. The normal UI reserves then immediately joins, but if the code reaches another participant first, the literal creator-first rule is not guaranteed. The cheap test uses real-time Bun.sleep polling and does not exercise the example's actual assignment parser or held-leader handoff by expiry through it.
